AB  - The genus Metarhizium (Ascomycota: Hypocreales) principally produces aerial conidia but can also produce microsclerotia (MS), which are resistant propagules that remain dormant under adverse environmental conditions. This tolerance makes MS promising candidates as mycoinsecticides for field application. In the present study, we compared the viability and virulence of aerial conidia and conidia derived from MS (CMS) of Metarhizium brunneum strain EAMa 01/58-Su under controlled laboratory conditions. Assessments included morphological (propagule size), physiological (germination rate and mycelial growth), biochemical (enzymatic profile and Pr1 protease) characteristics and insecticidal activity. These parameters were evaluated using culture-based methods and in vivo assays against third instar larvae (prepupae) of the model insect Ceratitis capitata Wied. (Diptera: Tephritidae). No differences were observed between size and shape of aerial conidia and CMS. However, significant differences were found in germination percentage and mycelial growth. Aerial conidia had a higher germination rate and linear mycelial growth, whereas CMS had a lower germination rate and a logarithmic trend in mycelial growth. While the types of enzymes produced by the two propagule types were similar, some differences in the intensity of enzymatic activity were detected. In vivo assays of both propagules against third-instar C. capitata larvae at four conidial concentrations demonstrated a dose-dependent increase in mortality with LC<sub>50</sub>'s of 2.68 × 10<sup>6</sup> and 1.32 × 10<sup>7</sup> conidia/mL for aerial conidia and CMS, respectively. Overall, our results indicate that aerial conidia of M. brunneum strain EAMa 01/58-Su are more suitable than CMS for soil dwelling insect control.
